{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The three consecutive even integers are 44, 46, and 48, making 48 the largest.","counterconditions":["If 138 were not divisible by 3, then the middle value would not be an integer and no such triple of consecutive even integers would exist.","If 138 divided by 3 yielded an odd number (e.g. 141/3 = 47), the resulting triple (45, 47, 49) would be odd, not even, and the even-constraint would fail.","If the problem did not require the integers to be even (allowing any consecutive integers), the answer would be different."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Let the middle even integer be x. The three consecutive even integers are x-2, x, and x+2. The sum is (x-2) + x + (x+2) = 3x. Setting 3x = 138 yields x = 46. The three integers are 44, 46, and 48. This works because the constant difference of 2 between consecutive even integers causes the -2 and +2 to cancel exactly, making the sum equal to three times the middle value.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","2316baad7b12326eaa49a37bacb6e6b07c11b9fe82018e24c78fe7496ed5397a","e7bb307edea5a70dc2260902793322c5be8265d5da5cfaac6d8073053a463e0d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.95}],"simulation_proposals":[]}